+# Refactoring
+
+## Activation contract
+
+Candidate capability: load only for an explicitly requested structural reorganization whose behavior-preservation risks warrant a dedicated procedure. Skip small local edits, feature changes, and simple renames already covered by code-organization and compatibility-migration. Do not create a new role. Stop when the scoped structural change is complete and characterization evidence has been compared with the baseline, or when a protected contract cannot be preserved. Expected output is a compact before/after contract map, changes, comparison evidence, and residual limits.
+
+## Procedure
+
+1. Before any structural mutation, establish the behavioral baseline from accepted observable behavior, public contracts, relevant consumers, stable identities, persistence/serialization, and references. Use existing tests, historical fixtures, or justified characterization checks and record unavailable runtime evidence.
+2. Resolve code-organization and applicable compatibility-migration rules. Freeze the intended ownership and dependency direction, protected identities, and allowed structural delta. Exclude opportunistic feature, dependency, and documentation changes.
+3. Move or split in small coherent steps, updating applicable consumers and references without recreating protected identity or data. Preserve rollback paths for each independent change.
+4. Compare fresh characterization evidence against the pre-change baseline. Green tests added after the move alone do not establish preserved behavior. Explain each intentional contract change separately; stop on unexplained differences or missing mandatory evidence.
+5. Report the preserved contracts, actual structural improvement, evidence boundary, and residual risk. This candidate enters Core only if isolated and removal-ablation comparisons demonstrate benefit beyond existing skills without material overhead on simple controls.
